## 1. Introduction
The excessive consumption of conventional fossil fuels such as coal, oil, and natural gas has resulted in significant energy shortages and environmental damage in today's society [1,2]. It is now more important than ever to create renewable energy sources in order to alleviate environmental stress [3]. Because of its high energy density, considerable energy conversion efficiency, and environmental friendliness, hydrogen is regarded as the most perfect alternative energy source among the numerous accessible [4,5]. Hydrogen may be produced by splitting water with electricity generated from renewable energy sources, which has shown to be an efficient approach [6,7]. However, the slow oxygen evolution reaction (OER), involving four consecutive proton-coupled electron transfers at the anode and relatively slow kinetics, limits efficient water splitting [8–10].
