Because of their high porosity, high specific surface area, and availability of metal active sites, metal organic backbones (MOFs) are regarded as competitive catalytic materials. However, most MOFs are not only weak electron conductors, but also have relatively unstable structures, making them problematic to use in large-scale electrocatalysis. Using MOFs as templates seems to be a reasonable technique for maximising the advantages of MOFs. T. I. Singh et al. [27] created a Fe-Co-oxide/Co-metal@N-doped carbon hybrid heterostructure grown on nickel foam electrocatalysts (Fe-Co-O/Co@NC/NF) by annealing Fe-Co-PBA in an Ar environment at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). A. M. Andrade et al. [28] pyrolyzed MOFs to create a Co/Cu-embedded \(N\)-doped carbon structure for triple-action catalysis of OER, ORR, and HER in a basic environment. Hengbo Wu et al. [29] developed an innovative and scalable strategy to prepare Co-Fe-P-Se nanoparticles loaded on two-dimensional nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets by the pyrolysis of MOF in the presence of phosphorus and selenium sources, which exhibited excellent catalytic performance in OER and ORR. However, the structure of the pristine MOF is destroyed by high-temperature calcination, which causes the metal centres to agglomerate at the cost of the molecular active sites. As a result, finding a gentle way to activate MOFs is critical.
In this research, we used a simple one-step alkali treatment to change Fe/Co/Ni-based MOF, which conserved the distinctive structure of MOF while also adding active sites to the surface. As shown in Scheme 1, the \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)in the solution reacts with the transition metal ions on the MOF's pores and surface to create hydroxide (MOF-OH). We explored the link between the base concentration and the structural and electrochemical characteristics of MOF-OH using Fe-MIL-80(A) as an example. As the KOH concentration increased from \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) to \(1.5\,\mathrm{M}\) (Fig. S1), the diffraction peaks of FeOOH gradually increased, implying stronger crystallinity. Fe
MIL-88(A)-OH demonstrated a considerable increase in catalytic activity in electrochemical experiments (Fig. S12). At a \(300\,\mathrm{mV}\) overpotential, the maximum current density of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH was \(22.56\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which was 83 times greater than that of pure Fe-MIL-88(A) \((0.27\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\). In 1 M KOH, Co-ZIF-67 and Ni-MOF-74 were similarly treated, yielding porous hydroxides \(\mathrm{CoO(OH)}\) (Co-ZIF-67-OH) and \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) (Ni-MOF-74-OH) with high catalytic activity, as expected.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Synthesis of Co-ZIF-67-OH, Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH and Ni-MOF-74-OH
The precursor Co-ZIF-67, Fe-MIL-88(A) and Ni-MOF-74 were prepared by referring to the synthesis method in the literature. In order to hydroxylate MOFs, Fe-MIL-88(A) \((150\,\mathrm{mg})\) were dispersed in 0.1 M KOH, 0.5 M KOH, 1 M KOH and \(1.5\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH, respectively, and the mixtures were stirred for \(7\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. Then the resultant brown turbid liquid was transferred to stainless steel autoclave lined with Teflon, and placed it in a muffle furnace to heat at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\). After cooling, the precipitates were collected by high-speed centrifugation, washed repeatedly with DI water and ethanol, and dried in a vacuum oven at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for overnight to obtain the \(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH}_{0.1}\), Fe-MIL-88(A)- \(\mathrm{OH}_{0.5}\), Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, and \(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH}_{1.5}\) samples, respectively. The preparations of Co-ZIF-67-OH and Ni-MOF-74-OH were done only with 1 M KOH and the procedure was similar to the above, except for substituting the Fe-MIL-88(A) with Co-ZIF-67 or Ni-MOF-74.
## 2.2. Materials characterizations
X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) was collected on a D/max-2500/ PC X-ray diffractometer with \(\mathrm{Cu}\,\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda\ =\ 1.5418\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\). Raman spectroscopy was measured using a Invia/Reflrx Laser Micro-Raman spectroscope (Horiba Jobin Yvon, French) with an equipped excitation laser beam wavelength of \(532\,\mathrm{nm}\) at room temperature. The morphology and elemental mapping were investigated on a scanning electron microscopy and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (JEOL JSM 7001F SEM and Oxford-Horiba Inca XMax50 EDX) with the accelerating voltage of \(20\,\mathrm{kV}\). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images were measured through a Hitachi H-7650 at \(100\,\mathrm{kV}\). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images and selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) were obtained on a Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN at \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\). XPS measurements were examined via a Thermo ESCALAB 250 X-ray Multifunctional imaging electron spectrometer.
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical activities were assessed on a CHI 760D electrochemistry workstation in a traditional three-electrode system at room temperature, using a graphite rod and a \(\mathrm{Hg}\ /\ \mathrm{HgO}\) as the counter electrode and the reference electrode, respectively. The prepared nickel foams were used as the working electrodes and the electrolyte was 1 M KOH. All potentials measured were transformed to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) based on the equation: \(E_{RHE}=E_{applied}+0.098\,\mathrm{V}+0.059\,\mathrm{pH}\). Where the \(E_{applied}\) is the applied potential. The LSV tests were performed at a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Tafel plots were obtained by replotting the polarization curves as overpotential \((\eta)\) versus the logarithm of current density \((\log\,|J|)\). CV tests were conducted in the non-Faradaic region with different sweeping rates to measure the electrochemical double-layer capacitance \((C_{dl})\). Accelerated durability tests of catalysts were conducted through continuous potential cycling ranged from 0.58 to 0.68 V vs. \(\mathrm{Hg}\ /\ \mathrm{HgO}\) for 3000 cycles at a scan rate of \(50\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\).
