## 3. Results and discussion
The structural changes of MOF before and after etching were depicted in Fig. 1a. In the case of Fe-MOF, owing to the corrosive effect of strong alkaline solution, the linking group between the metal ion and the organic ligand was broken when MOF was dispersed in KOH solution (MOF is unstable in strong alkaline solution). In \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)rich solutions, metal ions in MOFs gradually combine with \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)to produce (oxygen) hydronides directly, forming nanosheet structures on the surface. The nanosheet structure can promote the electron transfer rate, exposing a large number of metal ions and generating more active sites, which helped to improve the electrocatalytic property [30,31]. The transformation of the MOF structure was evidenced by other characterizations. The X-ray diffractions for the three MOF precursors are shown in the Fig. S2, and the diffraction peaks correspond to the reported single crystal data simulations of Co-ZIF-67 [32], Fe-MIL-88(A) [33], and Ni-MOF-74 [34], respectively. Fig. 1b illustrates that the MOF-derived compounds do not have the characteristic peaks of the original MOF, and all of them generate new diffraction peaks. The characteristic peaks of Co-ZIF-67-OH indexed well to CoO (OH) (JCPDS 07-0169) and \(\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}\) (JCPDS 30-0443), indicating that the derivative compound contains both \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\). The characteristic peaks of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH and Ni-MOF-74-OH correspond to \(\mathrm{FeO(OH)}\) (JCPDS 29-0713) and \(\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}\) (JCPDS 74-2075), respectively. In addition, we measured the Raman spectra of the three (oxy)hydroxide (Fig. 1c), and the two Raman bands of Co-ZIF-67-OH appeared at 531 and \(679\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which are consistent with the previously reported Raman spectra of cobalt oxyhydroxide [35]. The Raman peaks of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH at 241, 294, 393, 472, 557, 686 and \(1243\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) demonstrate that it is mainly composed of \(\mathrm{FeO(OH)}\) [36]. The Raman spectra of these two substances are consistent with the XRD results. For Ni-MOF-74-OH, its displacement at \(583\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be related to the stretching vibration of Ni-O, whereas the displacements at \(1285\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1421\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) represent the stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{O}\) and the bending vibration of \(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{OH}\), respectively [37].
The morphology of the obtained materials was inspected by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and the structural changes of Fe-MIL-88(A), Co-ZIF-67 and Ni-MOF-74 after hydroxylation were investigated (Fig. 2). Fig. 2a, e show that Fe-MIL-88 (A) and Ni-MOF-74 are both prismatic structures with lengths of \(2\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) and \(6\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\), respectively. For Co-ZIF-67, a typical hexagonal prism with a dimension of approximately \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) can be observed (Fig. 2c). After the low-temperature etching of the three precursors, their shapes basically remained unaltered, while the structure of the coordination polymer was dissolved into organic ligands and metals. The metal fraction combined with \(\mathrm{OH}-\) to form uniformly distributed layered transition metal hydroxide nanosheets on the structural surface (Fig. 2b, d and f) and retained the molecularly active sites of the MOF. Meanwhile, a distinct pore structure was observed in Ni-MOF-74-OH, indicating pore expansion during etching. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) further characterized the morphological changes of the samples. In comparison to the pristine MOF, nanosheets were formed on the surface of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH (Fig. S3), which was consistent with the results observed by TEM. This is beneficial to expose more active sites for Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, thus improving the catalytic activity of electrolytic water oxygen evolution. Similar results were obtained for two other derivatives, Co-ZIF-67-OH (Fig. S4) and Ni-MOF-74-OH (Fig. S7), demonstrating the universal applicability of etching MOF materials with KOH to obtain derived hydroxides. In addition,
the EDS-mapping shows that the elements in Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH (Fig. 3d-f), Co-ZIF-67-OH (Fig. S6) and Ni-MOF-74-OH (Fig. S9) are uniformly distributed. The high-resolution image corresponding to Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH illustrates that the lattice fringes are distributed in the same direction, and the interlayer distance of \(0.24~\mathrm{nm}\) corresponds to (111) of FeO(OH) (Fig. 3a, b). The related selection electron diffraction illustrates the single crystal properties of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, and the diffraction spots of \(0.24\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(0.40\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(0.20\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(0.17\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.26\,\mathrm{nm}\) can be assigned to (111), (110), (140), (221) and (021) of FeO(OH) respectively (Fig. 3c). These results are congruent with the XRD crystal structure data.
XPS was investigated to examine the surface chemistry of FeMIL-88(A) and Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH. The XPS survey spectra of all samples show the presence of Fe and O elements (Fig. 4a). Fig. 4b, c clearly illustrates the high-resolution XPS spectra of Fe 2p and O 1s regions. Specifically, in the Fe \(2\mathbf{p}\) spectrum of FeMIL-88(A), the binding energy peaks at \(711.4~\mathrm{eV}\) of Fe \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and \(725.0~\mathrm{eV}\) of Fe \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) are assigned to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), accompanied with three satellite features peaks (713.8 eV, 717.8 eV and 729.3 eV) [38]. Compared with Fe-MIL-88(A), the electron binding energy of FeMIL-88(A)-OH for \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) is transferred to a lower energy, indicating that the electron density of the Fe center is increased [39–40]. It has been demonstrated that the larger electron density in the Fe
center can promote the formation of hydroxide and thus enhance the OER activity [34]. The Ni 2p spectrum has two distinct peaks at \(855.9~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(873.5~\mathrm{eV}\), corresponding to Ni \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Ni \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\), respectively, which can be well attributed to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in Ni-MOF-74 [31]. Similar to Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, the electron binding energy of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in Ni-MOF-74-OH is also reduced (Fig. S11b). For the O element, \(\mathrm{O-H}\) is dominant in Fe-MIL-88(A) and Ni-MOF-74, while \(\mathrm{M-O}\) (\(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Fe}\), Ni) in Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH and Ni-MOF-74-OH is superior [19], indicating that metal–oxygen bonds are strengthened after etching (Fig. 4c and S11c). Co 2p of Co-ZIF-67 shows \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) (781.2 eV and 796.7 eV) and satellite peaks (786.3 eV and 802.3 eV), while \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) (780.0 eV and 795.0 eV), \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) (781.2 eV and 796.7 eV) and satellite peaks (786.9 eV and 804.0 eV) are generated by Co 2p from Co-ZIF-67-OH [42] (Fig. S10b). \(\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) is produced after hydroxylation treatment, which further confirms the formation of CoO(OH). O 1s can be divided into three different components: Co-O (530.1 eV), Co-H (531.2 eV), and N-O (532.5 eV) [43]. Compared with Co-ZIF-67, the peak strength of Co-O in Co-ZIF-67-OH is significantly enhanced, and the strength of N-O bond is significantly reduced (Fig. S10c), all of which are attributed to the generation of CoO(OH) and the disappearance of Co-ZIF-67. The peak strength of N 1s in Co-ZIF-67 is substantially higher than that of Co-ZIF-67-OH (Fig. S10d), demonstrating that the content of N element in Co-ZIF-67-OH is basically zero, and
Co-ZIF-67 is successfully converted into \(\mathrm{CoO(OH)}\) [43]. These findings further support the general application of producing transition metal hydroxides by etching MOF materials with KOH.
\(100~\mathrm{mV}\) more than Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH with \(407~\mathrm{mV}\), \(372~\mathrm{mV}\) and \(355\;\mathrm{mV}\), respectively. To attain the current density of \(500\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH requires \(388~\mathrm{mV}\) overpotential, while Fe-MIL-88 (A), \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) demand \(538\,\mathrm{mV}\), \(682\,\mathrm{mV}\) and \(732\,\mathrm{mV}\), respectively (Fig. 5a). The current density of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, Fe-MIL-88(A), \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\), and \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) at \(\eta\,=\,300\,\mathrm{mV}\) are 22.56, 0.27, 3.24 and \(1.37\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\), respectively, which is consistent with the trend of LSV curves (Fig. 5c). The OER dynamics information is evaluated using the Tafel slope. The Tafel slope of Fe-MIL-88 (A)-OH is \(50.76\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1}\), which is lower than that of Fe-MIL-88 (A) \((58.89\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\), \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\) \((79.96\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) \((95.27\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\), revealing that Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH has better reaction kinetics and faster charge transfer process on OER (Fig. 5b). Furthermore, in order to evaluate the relative electrochemically active surface area of the sample, we estimated the electrochemical double layer capacitance \((C_{dl})\) by cyclic voltammetry (CV). The \(C_{dl}\) values of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, Fe-MIL-88(A), \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) are obtained from the CV curves of different scan rates, which
are \(9.36\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\), \(1.15\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\), \(5.65\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\) and \(7.86\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\), respectively (Fig. 5d). This data indicates that Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH has a larger electrochemical surface area, which facilitates exposure of more active sites.
We also measured the electrolytic water oxygen evolution performance of other two MOFs and their derivatives. The overpotential of \(660\;\mathrm{mV}\) is required to achieve the current density of \(500\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\) for Co-ZIF-67-OH, which is significantly lower than that of Co-ZIF-67 (961 mV) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (1.62 V) (Fig. S13a). Correspondingly, the Tafel slope of Co-ZIF-67-OH (48.59 mV dec\(^{-1}\)) is also smaller than that of Co-ZIF-67 (65.61 mV dec\(^{-1}\)) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (118.94 mV dec\(^{-1}\)) (Fig. S13b). Co-ZIF-67-OH has a substantially greater electrochemical double layer capacitance (32.37 mF \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) than Co-ZIF-67 (6.96 mF \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (14.59 mF \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) (Fig. S15). Ni-MOF-74-OH requires \(813\;\mathrm{mV}\) potential to achieve a current density of \(500\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), which is remarkably superior than Ni-MOF-74 (1.05 V) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (1.28 V) (Fig. S14a). And their Tafel slope are \(60.98\;\mathrm{mV\;dec}^{-1}\), \(72.60\;\mathrm{mV\;dec}^{-1}\) and \(82.75\;\mathrm{mV\;dec}^{-1}\), respectively (Fig. S14b). Obviously, Ni-MOF-74-OH has better dynamic performance. Compared with Ni-MOF-74 (3.03 mF \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) (5.53 mF \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)), Ni-MOF-74-OH (6.48 mF \(\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\)) also has beneficial \(C_{dl}\) (Fig. S16). Figs. S21 and S22 illustrate the electrochemical properties of the three metallic materials. The three MOFs exhibit significant improvement in OER performance after hydroxylation, demonstrating the general applicability of this method. These results denote that the KOH-etched MOF will generate more exposed active sites, and thus its OER performance is considerably superior than that of MOF precursors. Meanwhile, the MOF-derived transition metal hydroxide involves organic ligands, resulting in higher activity than inorganic transition metal hydroxides. The as-prepared MOFs-OH materials also exhibit superior OER property than other
MOF-derived electrocatalysts (Table S1). In addition to catalytic performance, stability and long-term durability are key indicators. The polarization curves of Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH, Co-ZIF-67-OH and Ni-MOF-74-OH after \(3000\;\mathrm{CV}\) cycles were measured, and the original activity can still be maintained (Fig. 5e, S18). The LSV curves of the three original MOFs also did not degrade significantly after 3000 cycles (Fig. S19). The chronoamperometric curve reveals that Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH and Ni-MOF-74-OH could remain stable for \(30\;\mathrm{h}\) at a current density of \(50\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), and Co-ZIF-67-OH could remain stable within \(30\;\mathrm{h}\) at a current density of \(30\;\mathrm{mA\;cm}^{-2}\), indicating the high stability of these catalysts (Fig. 5f and S17). These data reveal that the hydroxylated MOF not only has high OER activity, but also has excellent durability. The structural stability of MOF-OH was confirmed using XRD. There was no significant variation in XRD before and after the OER test for Fe-MIL-88(A)-OH (Fig. S20).
As shown in Fig. S21, the electrochemical properties of three metallic materials were compared. The Fe-MOF-OH shows higher activity compared to the other transition metals, which can be due to the abundant \(-\mathrm{OOH}\), while Co-ZIF-67-OH contains a small amount of \(-\mathrm{OOH}\), Ni does not contain \(-\mathrm{OOH}\). Compared with \(-\mathrm{OH}\), the pendent O of \(-\mathrm{OOH}\) may cause additional interactions [44]. In addition, high-valent metal cations have a strong oxidizing ability, which will reduce the energy of the d-band and thus enhance the adsorption of oxygen-containing intermediates [45]. This also leads to better OER performance for Fe-MOF-OH.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, we have established a gentle etching approach for creating derived transition metal hydroxides that grow along
the surface and pore channels of MOFs. After etching cobalt-, iron-, and nickel-based MOFs in the same manner, similar products were produced. The current densities of the three compounds Fe-MIL-88 (A)-OH, Co-ZIF-67-OH, and Ni-MOF-74-OH achieved \(10\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\) with overpotentials of just \(278\ \mathrm{mV}\), \(255\ \mathrm{mV}\), and \(203\ \mathrm{mV}\), respectively, which were much better than those of the MOF precursor and inorganic hydroxide. Our study is an important guide for the modification of MOF and the synthesis of hydroxyl oxides.
